# The installer's stall floor: what was measured, what it says, what breaks it (2026-10-04)

Two open rows about `scripts/install.sh` are closed here. The first: the 153 MiB artifact is
fetched with no transfer limit at all, so a mirror that answers 200 and then trickles bytes
was waited on forever. The second: the success path names the URL that won and says nothing
about what the other candidates did, which is the information that decides whether to set
`CHAOS_GITHUB_MIRROR` by hand next time.

Everything below was measured on this machine with the tools the installer actually uses,
before the wording or the defaults were chosen. Section 8 reproduces it.

== 0. The hole, in the shipped call site

    $ grep -n 'download_github' scripts/install.sh
    294:download_github() {
    610:USED_URL="$(download_github "$ORIGIN_URL" "$TMP" 12 0 1048576)" || exit 1
    624:  if ! download_github "$SUMS_ORIGIN" "$sums_tmp" 10 30 >/dev/null; then
    668:  if ! download_github "$SIG_ORIGIN" "$sig_tmp" 10 30 >/dev/null 2>&1; then

Line numbers are as of this commit; the same grep on the file before the change reported
280, 530, 544 and 588. The arguments after `$TMP` are connect timeout, max-time, minimum
bytes. The artifact is the one call with max-time `0`, and the comment above it says why: a
cap short enough to help a bad line would also kill a legitimate slow one. The pair
`--speed-limit`/`--speed-time` is the shape that separates those two cases, because it bounds
the *rate* rather than the total time.

    $ grep -n -- '--max-time\|--speed-limit' scripts/install.sh
    305:  # a mirror that trickles bytes forever is waited on forever. --speed-limit aborts when
    322:    curl_args+=(--max-time "$max_time")
    325:    curl_args+=(--speed-limit "$min_bps" --speed-time "$stall_secs")
    380:      # curl reports 28 for the connect timeout, --max-time and the speed floor alike, so
    383:      # so with no --max-time armed the floor is the only thing that can have stopped it.
    432:    if curl -fsSL --connect-timeout 10 --max-time 30 -o "$tmp" "$cand" 2>/dev/null; then
    447:      curl -fsSLI --connect-timeout 10 --max-time 30 "$cand" 2>/dev/null \

The last two rows are the two tag lookups inside `latest_version()`, 30 s each. The sums and
signature fetches go through `download_github` with `max_time=30`, so the guarded append did
bound them. Only the artifact fetch passed `0`, and `--speed-limit` appeared nowhere in the
script before this change.

== 1. What was measured first

An endpoint that feeds fixed-size chunks with a fixed sleep between them, so the average rate
is what is under test rather than the scheduler. Every row fetches into a real file, for a
reason measured at the end of this section: writing to `/dev/null` changes curl's exit status
on abort from 28 to 23, which is the difference between "retried" and "given up".

    $ python3 <the probe in section 8>
    curl 7.81.0 (x86_64-pc-linux-gnu) libcurl/7.81.0 OpenSSL/3.0.2 zlib/1.2.11 brotli/1.0.9 zstd/1.4.8 libidn2/2.3.2 libpsl/0.21.0 (+libidn2 2.3.2) libssh/0.9.6/openssl/zlib nghttp2/1.43.0 librtmp/2.3 OpenLDAP/2.5.20
    floor used: --speed-limit 2048 --speed-time 2, on top of -fL --retry 2 --retry-delay 1 --connect-timeout 12
    stall  +floor    rc=28       http_code='200'   elapsed= 9.23s bytes=3072
    stall  nofloor   rc=timeout  http_code=''      elapsed=25.00s bytes=24576
    slowok +floor    rc=0        http_code='200'   elapsed= 7.02s bytes=1048576
    fast   +floor    rc=0        http_code='200'   elapsed= 0.01s bytes=1048576

Three facts came out of those four rows, and each one is load-bearing:

- Without the floor the trickle was still transferring when the probe gave up at 25 s. The
  release artifact's own `content-length` is 161233728 bytes (measured in
  `docker-lab-coverage-2026-10-04.log` §7), so at 1 KiB/s that is 43.7 hours of waiting, and
  the `min_bytes` floor is only evaluated once the transfer has ended. This is the defect.
- With the floor the abort exits **28 while `%{http_code}` still prints `200`**. Reading only
  the `-w` output turns a stalled mirror into an accepted download whose body then fails the
  size floor and is reported as `too small (3072 bytes)` -- a reason pointing at a truncated
  artifact when the mirror was stalled. So curl's exit status, not its status output, gates
  the decision.
- The same floor leaves a 128 KiB/s feed of 1 MiB completely alone: 7.02 s, all bytes, exit 0.
  This is why the guard is a rate floor and not a timeout.

Two probes then asked questions the wording depends on. The first: is the window armed only
while bytes are moving? It is not:

    $ python3 <the probe in section 8, block A>
    /slowhead  floor=1024  rc=28  out='000 1.201985' elapsed=5.61s
    /slowhead  floor=0     rc=0   out='200 3.004956' elapsed=3.02s
    /fast      floor=1024  rc=0   out='200 0.001823' elapsed=0.01s
    /fast      floor=0     rc=0   out='200 0.001817' elapsed=0.01s

`/slowhead` accepts the connection, sleeps 3 s before sending the response head, then serves
512 KiB at full speed; the window on those rows is `--speed-time 1`. It was aborted 1.20 s in
and `%{http_code}` said `000`, because no head had arrived to read a status from. With the
floor off, the same fetch finished in 3.02 s. So `--speed-time` counts from the start of the
transfer, before the first byte, which means the floor also bounds "the mirror accepted and
then said nothing" -- a case previously bounded only by `--max-time`, which the artifact fetch
does not set. That is why the reason line for a 0-byte body says `no bytes in Ns` rather than
`stalled under N B/s`: the second would describe a transfer that never started.

The second probe is the reason every row above fetches into a real file. Same trickle, same
floor, three destinations:

    $ python3 <the probe in section 8, block B>
    /dev/null    rc=23  http_code=     elapsed= 2.01s bytes_at_dest=0
    real file    rc=28  http_code=200  elapsed= 8.03s bytes_at_dest=384
    missing dir  rc=23  http_code=200  elapsed= 0.01s bytes_at_dest=0

`-o /dev/null` turns that same abort into exit 23 (`CURLE_WRITE_ERROR`) and curl writes
nothing at all for `-w`, so there is no status to report. The two rows also disagree about
`--retry`: the real-file row took 8.03 s because exit 28 is retried (`--retry 2 --retry-delay
1` is 2+1+2+1+2 s of windows and pauses), while the `/dev/null` row took 2.01 s, one attempt,
because 23 is not in the retryable set. So the destination curl writes to decides both the
exit code and whether the mirror loop gets a second candidate at all -- which is why the
fixture and this probe both use a real file, and why the reason text is keyed off the exit
status of a fetch that was actually writing bytes. The third row is what a genuine write
failure looks like, for comparison: 0.01 s, no bytes, the same 23.

== 2. What the shipped function does now

Four changes inside `download_github`, all of them on the paths described above:

- Two environment variables, `CHAOS_DOWNLOAD_MIN_BPS` (default `1024`) and
  `CHAOS_DOWNLOAD_STALL_SECS` (default `45`), appended as `--speed-limit`/`--speed-time` when
  the floor is above zero. `0` removes the floor. Non-numeric input falls back to the default
  rather than reaching `[[ -gt ]]`, where under `set -u` a bare word is an "unbound variable"
  abort in the middle of the mirror loop.
- curl's exit status gates the verdict: a `200` carried out of a non-zero exit becomes `000`,
  so the accepted-body branch is unreachable for an aborted transfer. A real `404` is
  untouched.
- The reason for exit 28 names the timers that were armed instead of guessing which fired, and
  distinguishes the one case bytes can settle: with bytes in hand and no `--max-time`, the
  floor is the only thing that can have stopped it, so that line says `stalled under 1024 B/s
  for 45s (3072 bytes in 9s)`.
- The success path prints the deduplicated reasons for the candidates it skipped, as `
  skipped:` lines on stderr, before printing the winning URL. The failure path already printed
  them as `  why:` lines; only the success path was silent.

Both copies of the help text document the two variables. The `--help` output for the piped
form (`curl ... | bash -s -- --help`) is a heredoc, because there is no file to read; the file
form used to print `sed -n '2,30p' "$0"`, a line range that has to be bumped in step with
every added header line and silently truncates when it is not. It now reads the leading
comment block with `awk` and stops at the first non-comment line.

== 3. The regression the existing guard caught while this was being written

The first version forced `http_code=000` whenever curl exited non-zero. `scripts/ci/
test-installer-download-size.py`, which is not about stalls at all, went red immediately:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-installer-download-size.py
    FAIL: test_missing_object_reports_the_status_code (__main__.DownloadGithubSizeTests)
    AssertionError: None is not true : no status reason:   try: http://127.0.0.1:42177/absent.bin
    error: download failed for http://127.0.0.1:42177/absent.bin
      why: curl exit 22 from http://127.0.0.1:42177/absent.bin
      last: curl exit 22 from http://127.0.0.1:42177/absent.bin
      tip: set CHAOS_GITHUB_MIRROR=https://ghfast.top  or  CHAOS_CN=1

`curl -f` exits 22 on a 404, and the status code is the one fact worth reporting there.
Replacing it with the curl exit number made the report worse in the ordinary case in order to
fix the stalled case. The shipped condition is `curl_rc != 0 && http_code == "200"`, and the
reason chain checks for a real status before it considers the exit code. All five size
fixtures pass again:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-installer-download-size.py
    Ran 5 tests in 2.638s

    OK

The same probe also surfaced a documentation drift that no gate had been looking at: the
header comment block documented `CHAOS_VERSION` and the three mirror variables but not
`CHAOS_SKIP_CHECKSUM`, which the piped `--help` has always documented. A reader who opened the
file with `less scripts/install.sh` never saw the checksum escape hatch exist. It is in the
header now, and `test_env_names_documented_in_the_header_match_the_heredoc` compares the
variable names between the two renderings in both directions.

== 4. The fixtures

`scripts/ci/test-installer-download-stall.py` runs the shipped `download_github`, extracted
verbatim from `install.sh` with the same extractor `test-installer-download-size.py` uses (it
is imported, not copied), against a paced HTTP server on 127.0.0.1. Only
`github_url_candidates` is stubbed, because the real list resolves github.com. `min_bytes` is
1 in every harness, so no refusal below can be the size floor's doing.

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-installer-download-stall.py
    test_both_help_paths_document_the_floor ... ok
    test_dead_candidate_is_named_on_the_success_path ... ok
    test_env_names_documented_in_the_header_match_the_heredoc ... ok
    test_floor_is_read_from_the_environment_with_these_names ... ok
    test_floor_of_zero_leaves_the_transfer_running ... ok
    test_reason_report_is_capped_at_four_lines ... ok
    test_repeated_reason_is_reported_once ... ok
    test_silent_endpoint_reports_no_bytes_not_slow_bytes ... ok
    test_stalled_candidate_is_named_when_the_next_one_works ... ok
    test_transfer_above_the_floor_finishes ... ok
    test_trickle_is_aborted_and_named_as_a_stall ... ok
    Ran 11 tests in 30.270s

    OK

What each of the four behavioural ones pins:

- `test_trickle_is_aborted_and_named_as_a_stall`: 128 B/s against the 1024 floor must abort,
  and the report must say `stalled under 1024 B/s for 1s from` followed by the byte count it
  had in hand and the seconds it took, and must
  must *not* contain `too small`. It also asserts the transfer ended in under 30 s and left no
  body behind.
- `test_transfer_above_the_floor_finishes`: 64 KiB at 16 KiB/s, watched over a 3 s window it
  outlasts, must land with all bytes. Without this case, "the floor aborts" could be satisfied
  by a floor that aborts everything.
- `test_silent_endpoint_reports_no_bytes_not_slow_bytes`: the 200-then-silence endpoint from
  section 1, asserting `no bytes in` and the absence of `stalled under`.
- `test_floor_of_zero_leaves_the_transfer_running`: `CHAOS_DOWNLOAD_MIN_BPS=0` with no
  max-time leaves nothing watching, which is observed as the harness still downloading when it
  is cut off at 6 s. It runs in its own process group and kills the group, because killing
  only the shell leaves curl holding the captured pipe open and the parent waiting on a
  transfer that never ends.

== 5. Mutation matrix

A driver mutates `install.sh` itself, one anchor at a time, and re-runs the tests that should
notice. It refuses to touch anything unless the baseline is green, and restores with a plain
`cp` followed by `cmp` in a `finally` block.

    == baseline (18.61s) rc=0
    Ran 6 tests in 18.551s
    OK
    == M1 floor never armed -> KILLED (120.25s rc=1)
         test_trickle_is_aborted_and_named_as_a_stall ... ERROR
         test_silent_endpoint_reports_no_bytes_not_slow_bytes ... ERROR
         restored byte-identical
    == M2 curl exit no longer gates the 200 -> KILLED (5.58s rc=1)
         test_trickle_is_aborted_and_named_as_a_stall ... FAIL
         reason: AssertionError: 0 == 0 : stall went unwatched:   try: http://127.0.0.1:40847/stall
         restored byte-identical
    == M3 success path says nothing about the others -> KILLED (6.17s rc=1)
         test_stalled_candidate_is_named_when_the_next_one_works ... FAIL
         test_dead_candidate_is_named_on_the_success_path ... FAIL
         restored byte-identical
    == M4 floor defaults to 0 (disabled by default) -> KILLED (1.06s rc=1)
         test_floor_is_read_from_the_environment_with_these_names ... FAIL
         restored byte-identical
    == driver done, 0 problem(s)

M1's two `ERROR`s are `subprocess.TimeoutExpired`: with the flags removed, nothing aborts,
which is the original defect reproduced rather than asserted. M2 is the interesting one --
with the exit gate gone and `min_bytes` at 1, the harness *accepts* the stalled 384-byte body
and exits 0, so the test's first assertion is on the status and not only on the wording.

== 6. The same behaviour inside a container with no route to the network

`scripts/ci/release-integrity-serve.py` gained `--throttle CASE=BYTES_PER_SEC`, repeatable,
which paces that scenario's responses. Two scenarios were added to
`scripts/install-integrity-in- docker.sh` by copying the `good` release directory twice, so
the bytes, the digest and the signature are identical to the scenario that installs cleanly
and only the endpoint's pacing differs. Smoke test of the pacing, against the shipped server
on this host:

    good   rc=0 elapsed=0.02s bytes=1048577
    slow   rc=0 elapsed=4.04s bytes=1048577
    stall  rc=28 elapsed=3.04s bytes=372

Then the lab, in a `--network none` container:

    $ bash scripts/install-integrity-in-docker.sh
    == what a mirror that is up and useless does
       ok  stall: refused a mirror serving 128 B/s after 19s (exit 1) -- why: stalled under 1024
    B/s for 3s from http://127.0.0.1:8099/stall/https://
       ok  stall: the artifact was really requested, so this is a transfer that was watched and
    not a 404 in disguise
       ok  stall: nothing installed behind the refusal
       ok  slow: the same artifact at 262144 B/s installs in 4s, past the 3s window, checksum and
    signature both verified
       ok  slow: what landed runs and reports 9.9.9
    ...
    all 40 release-integrity checks passed

Every case in that lab now runs under `timeout 300` inside the container. Without it a
regression in the floor turns the stall scenario into a 1 MiB download at 128 B/s, which is
hours, and the lab would read as slow rather than as wrong; the stall assertion also checks
that the exit code is not 124, so a passing case is the installer deciding and not the leash.

The lab's own stall case was then checked for the ability to fail, by dropping the
`--throttle` flag for its scenario so the endpoint serves those bytes at full speed:

    == mutant run rc=1 in 35.0s
       stderr  - stall: exited 0; the artifact arrived and the installer never objected to the rate
       stderr  - stall: refused, yet bin/chaos is present and runnable
    == lab NOTICED the un-throttled mirror
    restored byte-identical

== 7. Gate legs

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-script-portability.py
    script-portability: OK (24 shell script(s))
    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-pipefail-report.py
    pipefail-report: OK (31 shell script(s), 23 with `set -e` scanned, 0 hazard assignment(s))
    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-guard-wiring.py
    check-guard-wiring: OK (70 files in scripts/ci/, 69 reachable, 65 run by scripts/verify-in-
    docker.sh, 4 recorded CI-only, 1 exempt)
    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-lab-coverage.py
    check-lab-coverage: OK (6 lab(s): 3 run by CI, 3 with a current ledger row)

The new fixture is wired on an executable line of both roots: appended to the `installer
guards` entry of `scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` and as its own step in
`.github/workflows/ci.yml`, which is what `check-guard-wiring.py` reads.

The container leg for that same entry, with the new fixture inside it:

    $ ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "installer guards"
    Ran 11 tests in 30.292s

    OK

    selected gates passed in chaos-verify:local
      --only was in effect: 1 of 37 gates ran, so this is not a full sweep

The `37` is what the entry point counts outside `--full`: the two cargo-appended gates are
added only in full mode, so a full sweep runs 39 and `--only` reports the 37 it selects from.
The suite that ran inside that gate is the same 11 tests as section 4, plus the four other
installer fixtures the entry bundles (14 + 8 + 5 + 11 tests).

== 8. Reproduce

The fixture is the durable form of every measurement above; the raw curl numbers are not
needed to check the conclusions, but they are reproducible without this log:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-installer-download-size.py
    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-installer-download-stall.py
    $ bash scripts/install-integrity-in-docker.sh

To re-measure the four curl rows, paste this into a shell. It is the probe used in section 1,
in one piece: an endpoint that feeds 1 KiB/s on `/trickle` and 128 KiB/s on `/paced`, fetched
twice each, with and without the floor.

    $ python3 - <<'PY'
    import http.server, subprocess, tempfile, threading, time, os
    B = 1024 * 1024
    ROUTES = {"/trickle": (B * 8, 1024, 1.0), "/paced": (B, 128 * 1024, 1.0)}

    class H(http.server.BaseHTTPRequestHandler):
        protocol_version = "HTTP/1.1"
        def log_message(self, *a): return None
        def do_GET(self):
            total, chunk, pause = ROUTES[self.path]
            self.send_response(200)
            self.send_header("Content-Length", str(total))
            self.end_headers()
            sent = 0
            try:
                while sent < total:
                    self.wfile.write(b"z" * min(chunk, total - sent)); self.wfile.flush()
                    sent += chunk
                    time.sleep(pause)
            except OSError:
                return

    srv = http.server.ThreadingHTTPServer(("127.0.0.1", 0), H)
    threading.Thread(target=srv.serve_forever, daemon=True).start()
    dest = os.path.join(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="chaos-curl-stall-"), "body")
    for label, route, floor, bound in [
            ("stall  +floor", "/trickle", True, 20), ("stall  nofloor", "/trickle", False, 25),
            ("slowok +floor", "/paced", True, 60)]:
        args = (["curl", "-fL", "--retry", "2", "--retry-delay", "1", "--connect-timeout", "12",
                 "-o", dest, "-w", "%{http_code}"]
                + (["--speed-limit", "2048", "--speed-time", "2"] if floor else [])
                + [f"http://127.0.0.1:{srv.server_address[1]}{route}"])
        if os.path.exists(dest):
            os.remove(dest)
        t0 = time.monotonic()
        try:
            p = subprocess.run(args, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=bound)
            code, rc = p.stdout.strip(), p.returncode
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            code, rc = "", "timeout"
        print(f"{label:16s} rc={str(rc):<8s} http_code={code!r:<7s} "
              f"elapsed={time.monotonic() - t0:5.2f}s bytes={os.path.getsize(dest)}")
    srv.shutdown()
    PY

The two probes in the tail of section 1 are this one script; block A prints the slow-head
rows, block B the three destinations. It serves a 128 B/s trickle, so it takes about 20 s.

    $ python3 - <<'PY'
    import http.server, os, subprocess, tempfile, threading, time

    SLOW_BODY = b"z" * (512 * 1024)
    STALL_RATE = 128
    STALL_BODY = bytes(STALL_RATE * 20)

    class H(http.server.BaseHTTPRequestHandler):
        protocol_version = "HTTP/1.1"
        def log_message(self, *a): return None
        def do_GET(self):
            paced = self.path == "/stall"
            if self.path == "/slowhead":
                time.sleep(3)              # connection up, no response head for 3 s
            body = STALL_BODY if paced else SLOW_BODY
            self.send_response(200)
            self.send_header("Content-Length", str(len(body)))
            self.end_headers()
            try:
                if paced:
                    for i in range(0, len(body), STALL_RATE):
                        self.wfile.write(body[i:i + STALL_RATE]); self.wfile.flush()
                        time.sleep(1)
                else:
                    self.wfile.write(body)
            except OSError:
                pass

    srv = http.server.ThreadingHTTPServer(("127.0.0.1", 0), H)
    threading.Thread(target=srv.serve_forever, daemon=True).start()
    port = srv.server_address[1]
    BASE = ["curl", "-fL", "--retry", "2", "--retry-delay", "1", "--connect-timeout", "12",
            "-w", "%{http_code} %{time_total}"]

    for path in ("/slowhead", "/fast"):          # A: armed before the first byte?
        for floor in (1024, 0):
            args = list(BASE) + ["-o", os.devnull]
            if floor:
                args += ["--speed-limit", str(floor), "--speed-time", "1"]
            args.append("http://127.0.0.1:%d%s" % (port, path))
            t0 = time.monotonic()
            p = subprocess.run(args, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
            print(f"{path:10s} floor={floor:<5d} rc={p.returncode:<3d} out={p.stdout.strip()!r} "
                  f"elapsed={time.monotonic() - t0:.2f}s")

    dest = os.path.join(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="chaos-curl-dest-"), "body")
    for name, out in ((os.devnull, os.devnull), ("real file", dest),
                      ("missing dir", "/nonexistent-dir-x/y.bin")):
        if os.path.exists(dest):
            os.remove(dest)
        args = list(BASE) + ["--speed-limit", "2048", "--speed-time", "2", "-o", out,
                             "http://127.0.0.1:%d/stall" % port]
        t0 = time.monotonic()
        p = subprocess.run(args, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        have = os.path.getsize(dest) if os.path.exists(dest) else 0
        code = p.stdout.split()[0] if p.stdout else ""
        print(f"{name:12s} rc={p.returncode:<3d} http_code={code:4s} "
              f"elapsed={time.monotonic() - t0:5.2f}s bytes_at_dest={have}")
    srv.shutdown()
    PY

The timing columns vary from run to run; the exit codes, the status strings and the byte
counts do not. `http_code=` is empty on the `/dev/null` row because curl wrote nothing at all
for `-w` on that row.

The first version of this script was wrong in a way worth naming, because the same mistake
silently voids whatever the probe claims to have measured: it chose the paced branch with
`self.path != "/slowhead"`, which made `/fast` a trickle route as well, and the two "fast"
rows then measured the slow endpoint. The tell was `/fast floor=1024 rc=23` -- a row existing
to show an untouched transfer coming back aborted. Select the paced branch by the route's own
name, and read the control row before believing the experimental one.

The mutation drivers in sections 5 and 6 are not committed: they mutate tracked files, which
is the one thing a repository should not keep a recipe for. Their structure is stated in this
log because that is the part worth keeping -- anchor must appear exactly once, baseline must
be green before anything is edited, restore with `cp` then `cmp`, in a `finally`.